FreeSDN database runtime: upgrading, and the exceptional migration path
======================================================================

Upgrading from 26.09.0 to 26.10.0 is an IMAGE-ONLY update. Pull the new images
and start the stack. There is no logical migration, no new volume, no locale
change and no downtime beyond the restart. The procedure in the second half of
this file is NOT part of that upgrade.

This file previously documented a Debian -> Alpine transition. That transition
was not shipped: 26.10.0 stays on the Debian runtime precisely so that the
ordinary upgrade stays an image swap. The statements it carried about Alpine
3.24, the builtin C.UTF-8 locale provider and postgres UID 70 were true only of
that withdrawn candidate and are corrected below.

The ordinary upgrade
--------------------
The shipped images use PostgreSQL 18 on Debian trixie, with rebuilt gosu and
supported OS updates. LogDB contains checksum-pinned TimescaleDB 2.30.2, with
the 2.30.1 versioned library and install SQL retained for exact logical
restores. Fresh databases select 2.30.2. A restored database retains its source
extension version; the migration tool does not upgrade or downgrade it
implicitly. Extension upgrades remain a separate reviewed operation on the
recovered target, never on the sole source copy.

New clusters use data checksums. The locale provider, collation and the
postgres OS UID/GID (999) are unchanged from 26.09.0, which is what makes the
existing volume directly reusable.

What the entrypoint does to an existing volume
----------------------------------------------
The entrypoint records a runtime marker, FREESDN_RUNTIME, inside PGDATA, and
decides before upstream's startup or any ownership change:

  * No marker, PostgreSQL 18: ADOPTED. It logs the adoption, writes the marker,
    and reads, rewrites or converts nothing. Every cluster created by a release
    older than the marker -- including every 26.09.0 deployment -- is in this
    state, because the marker is a FreeSDN invention that those releases could
    not have written. An operator who knows their unmarked cluster is NOT a
    glibc cluster can set FREESDN_REFUSE_UNMARKED=1 to force a refusal instead.
  * Marker present and matching: started normally, with no further action.
  * Marker present and DIFFERENT (for example a cluster initialised by the
    withdrawn Alpine candidate): REFUSED, exit 78.
  * A different PostgreSQL major version, or another cluster layout in the
    volume: REFUSED, exit 78.

Collation is deliberately not consulted. PostgreSQL 16 removed LC_COLLATE and
LC_CTYPE from both the server GUC and pg_controldata output, so a guard that
tried to read one back would compare empty strings and refuse every valid
cluster while appearing careful.

Do not manufacture this marker. It is an accidental-upgrade guard, not an
access-control boundary against a Docker administrator. Do not override the
entrypoint or use a different data_directory to bypass it.

When the exceptional path applies
---------------------------------
Everything from here on is for a cluster the guard REFUSES, which on a normal
26.09.0 -> 26.10.0 upgrade does not happen. It is an OFFLINE LOGICAL migration,
not an image-only update: read the entire procedure and rehearse on a restored
copy before changing a customer deployment. Keep a verified encrypted backup
and the old source images, configuration, secrets and volumes. Never run two
servers against the same data directory.

Supported automatic logical-copy scope
--------------------------------------
scripts/migrate_database_runtime.py supports PostgreSQL 18 -> equal/newer 18,
one application database per cluster plus an empty postgres maintenance DB,
UTF8, named Docker volumes and matching extension versions. Both containers
must use --network none, no published ports, local socket authentication and
the SAME initdb bootstrap role (OID 10, normally freesdn). Target initialization
must use POSTGRES_DB=postgres, not the application database name.

Matching bootstrap identities matter: PostgreSQL 16+ role grants retain a
grantor that cannot be substituted by an arbitrary new superuser. The tool
restores roles, attributes, original password hashes and memberships. It skips
only the already-existing bootstrap role's exact CREATE ROLE statement.

Custom tablespaces, foreign tables, subscriptions, replication slots, prepared
transactions, explicit nonportable libc collations, extra application databases
missing extension versions and Timescale installed outside public are refused. Review these cases separately;
never delete them solely to bypass preflight. In HA, fence/remove the old
standby from service and rebuild a NEW standby from the migrated primary. Do
not reconnect an old physical standby to the new runtime.

This tool creates the recovered database with PostgreSQL's builtin C.UTF-8
locale provider (migrate_database_runtime.py: CREATE DATABASE ... LOCALE_PROVIDER
builtin BUILTIN_LOCALE 'C.UTF-8'), whatever the source used. That was chosen
for a musl target and is retained, so a migration OUT OF a glibc en_US.utf8
cluster DOES change the default locale even though the shipped runtime is still
glibc. Text sorting, case conversion and expression/unique index behaviour can
differ from en_US.utf8. Indexes are rebuilt by logical restore and pg_amcheck
verifies them, which does not prove that every application relies on the same
sorting. This is why --accept-locale-change cannot be omitted, and it is a
further reason not to use this path for an upgrade the entrypoint would simply
adopt.
Review custom queries/constraints and test application behavior. The explicit
--accept-locale-change acknowledgement cannot be omitted.

Offline workflow
----------------
1. Record exact old image IDs, volume names and compose configuration privately.
   Verify both encrypted database backups by restoring them into an isolated
   environment. Budget space for original volumes, new volumes, logical dumps,
   sort/temp files and a safety margin. Fingerprinting reads and sorts all user
   rows; large deployments need a measured maintenance window.
2. Stop ALL application writers (API, workers, scheduler, collector, plugins,
   backup/maintenance clients) and BOTH database services. Stop/fence HA peers.
   Do not use `docker compose down -v`, prune, or delete old volumes.
3. Start an isolated source helper using the EXACT old image ID, the old named
   volume mounted at /var/lib/postgresql, --network none, no port publishing,
   and command: postgres -c default_transaction_read_only=on
   For LogDB also pass -c shared_preload_libraries=timescaledb and
   -c timescaledb.max_background_workers=0. Preserve its original PGDATA.
   Never start this helper while another server uses the same volume.
4. Create a NEW named target volume. Start the new FreeSDN database image using
   --network none, that new volume at /var/lib/postgresql, POSTGRES_DB=postgres,
   and POSTGRES_USER matching the original bootstrap role. Supply a temporary
   initialization password through a private --env-file; do not use trust host
   authentication or put real passwords into command history. The restore will
   replace the temporary password hash with the original source role hash.
   For LogDB pass -c shared_preload_libraries=timescaledb and
   -c timescaledb.max_background_workers=0 on this helper too.
   Do not attach application init scripts to the target helper.
5. Run read-only preflight, separately for primary and LogDB:

   python3 scripts/migrate_database_runtime.py \
     --source old-primary-helper --target new-primary-helper \
     --source-user freesdn --database freesdn

   Substitute the actual helper/user/database names. Use --database freesdn_logs
   for the usual LogDB database. Resolve every refusal before execution.
6. Re-run with --execute --accept-locale-change and a NEW absolute --work-dir on
   trusted POSIX storage. The script creates it with mode 0700 and files 0600:

   python3 scripts/migrate_database_runtime.py \
     --source old-primary-helper --target new-primary-helper \
     --source-user freesdn --database freesdn \
     --execute --accept-locale-change \
     --work-dir /secure-private-storage/primary-runtime-migration

   The directory contains database.dump and roles.sql, including confidential
   data and password hashes. Protect/encrypt it; never attach it to an issue or
   publish it as release evidence. Receipts contain metadata/content digests,
   not row contents. This is a migration workspace, not the encrypted backup.
7. Require exit 0 AND receipt.json with verified=true for BOTH databases. The
   tool checks original and restored rows, sequences, large objects, role and
   permission metadata, Timescale objects/jobs, checksums and indexes. It also
   checks the source again for changes. Timescale restore is serial, using
   timescaledb_pre_restore()/timescaledb_post_restore(). A failed target is
   incomplete: preserve diagnostics and retry into a NEW disposable target.
   The tool never drops databases, deletes volumes or switches the application.
8. Stop both helper containers. Record and review a compose override selecting
   the NEW primary/LogDB volumes by their exact names (external: true). Keep old
   volumes and the old configuration. Rebuild HA standby on another NEW volume.
   Start the new databases with normal configuration, without the offline
   read-only/background-worker restrictions. Start application services in
   maintenance/read-only mode, verify login, migrations, queries, background
   processing, log ingestion and encrypted backup/restore before admitting users.
   Only then resume production writes. Match every service's credentials to the
   restored roles. Environment POSTGRES_PASSWORD does not reset an existing DB.

I/O budgets and interrupted operations
-------------------------------------
The tool bounds captured SQL output plus diagnostics to4MiB per command and
in-memory SQL/role input to16MiB. Metadata must be unambiguous JSON rows with
at most64 levels of nesting. These are memory limits, not dump-size limits.
Roles exports are also limited to16MiB before reading or rewriting their exact
bootstrap CREATE ROLE statement. Review larger role catalogues separately.

Dump/restore inputs and streamed fingerprint files have a default64GiB ceiling
per file; the combined migration workspace defaults to128GiB. The flags
--file-limit-bytes and --workspace-limit-bytes set positive finite limits up to
4TiB. The work directory may contain only the tool's known private regular
files. Dumps, roles, temporary fingerprints and receipts all count toward its
budget. Do not place unrelated files there or run multiple writers against it.

The free-space reserve defaults to the smaller of4GiB or one quarter of the
filesystem capacity. --min-free-bytes sets an explicit positive reserve. This
is checked as output is written; it is not a disk reservation against other
processes. Reserve additional space for database volumes, server-side sorting,
WAL and other workloads: those are outside the host-workspace quota.

--timeout remains the maximum per database command (positive, at most24hours).
--operation-timeout additionally bounds the whole migration or preflight;
it defaults to6hours, with a maximum7days. Fingerprint stream and hash share
one command deadline. Time spent in filesystem workers counts toward these
limits; deadline cleanup may take the supervisor's additional cleanup grace.

Named binary file streams must be regular, single-linked, owned by the current
user and inside a private owned directory. Pending buffered output is refused.
The worker inherits pinned descriptors instead of reopening a path; identity,
file position and modification metadata are checked during/after I/O. Maintain
exclusive ownership: these checks are not protection against a privileged
filesystem or Docker administrator, or every hostile same-user writer.

Blocking reads/writes/hashes execute in a separately supervised process group.
No client timeout promises to cancel or roll back work already accepted by the
Docker daemon. Kernel uninterruptible I/O, process creation itself, escaped
process groups and external child reapers need independent host containment.
A failed attempt retains private partial files and an unqualified target.
Never resume it in place or mistake receipt.pending for a completed receipt.
Require both exit0 and the final receipt.json; interruption during final atomic
receipt publication can leave a receipt despite an uncertain caller outcome.
Preserve that first outcome and inspect/reconcile rather than blindly retrying.

Rollback and limits
-------------------
Before admitting writes: stop the candidate services/helpers and restart the
old exact images/configuration on their untouched original volumes. The old
source helper was logically read-only; normal PostgreSQL startup/checkpoint
files may still have changed. No physical copy to the new runtime is performed.
After admitting writes: simply returning to the old volume loses those writes.
Use a reviewed reverse logical migration/data reconciliation and backup plan.
Never promise zero RPO or instant rollback after cutover.

Custom postgresql.conf, pg_hba.conf, certificates, role authorization policy,
replication slots and external dependencies are operational configuration. The
logical tool does not copy these files across runtimes. Review and reapply them
deliberately. Verification is not a claim of identical locale semantics.

Repeatable disposable qualification
-----------------------------------
Run scripts/check_database_runtime.py against the old and candidate images.
Run scripts/check_database_ha.py for streaming/recreation/manual promotion and
scripts/restore_drill.sh with POSTGRES_IMAGE/LOGDB_IMAGE set to the built images
for encrypted backup recovery and rejection of damaged/missing-key restores.
docker/qualification/Dockerfile.legacy-logdb is an offline CI source fixture,
NOT a deployment or release image. Never publish it as a supported runtime.

The runtime checker now requires an unused report name in an existing directory
owned by the invoking user and not writable by a group or other users. Symlink
path components and existing reports are refused before engine contact. It also
exclusively creates REPORT.artifacts alongside the report; an existing attempt
directory is not reused, even if its final report is missing.

The artifact directory is private. It retains the initial intent, append-only
resource plans and observations, migration work, bounded private service logs,
and the first outcome. Treat it as sensitive: role dumps can contain password
hashes. Do not publish it with ordinary CI logs. No automatic filesystem cleanup
removes it. Review and archive it before any deliberate retention cleanup.

Image references must already exist locally. The checker records their resolved
Linux image IDs and uses those IDs with --pull=never for disposable containers.
It does not build, pull, scan or sign images. Separate image build/provenance and
vulnerability qualification remain required. Both normal and guard containers
have memory, swap, CPU, PID and log bounds; no ports or external container network
are enabled. This is a synthetic migration fixture, not a customer-scale test.

--timeout-seconds controls work, from a positive value up to the default 900
seconds. Database operations share that deadline. Evidence collection and cleanup
have separate 60/120-second budgets so work cannot renew its lifetime through a
retry loop. Local descriptor acquisition and filesystem synchronization are
ordinary OS calls, not hard realtime guarantees. An external watchdog still
needs to reconcile daemon work: killing a Docker client does not prove that the
engine cancelled its command.

SIGINT and SIGTERM request a failed qualification. The command supervisor stops
work and terminates its owned client process group; bounded evidence collection
and resource reconciliation may continue. Repeated signals do not interrupt
first-result writes or renew cleanup deadlines. Original signal handlers are
restored before returning to an embedding caller. Require the actual process
exit status: a signal during final publication may leave a report whose earlier
snapshot cannot represent that late interruption.

SIGKILL, host failure and a privileged engine/filesystem changing underneath the
checker are outside cooperative cancellation. Retained intent is not proof that
daemon resources were removed. An independent owner must reconcile such an
uncertain attempt before another qualification uses its resources.

Resource plans are persisted before creation. Lost acknowledgements and failed
inspection remain uncertainty. Cleanup verifies exact planned names, labels and
known container identities; it does not prune shared resources or infer absence
from an inspection error. A trusted engine administrator and exclusive ownership
of the evidence paths are required. Volumes do not have immutable Docker IDs.
For an interrupted process, use the retained intent and events to inspect only
that attempt's resources; never rerun into the same targets or sweep by prefix.

Require exit 0, a newly published report with qualification_complete=true, reviewed
artifact hashes and reviewed cleanup. An internal outcome alone is insufficient:
exclusive report publication or synchronization can fail after it is written.
The checker retains snapshots and bounded logs; these do not replace full engine
event, production deployment, hardware, recovery-time or high-availability gates.

